.TH gvm-manage-certs "1" "2015-09-21" "The OpenVAS Project" "User commands"
.SH NAME
gvm-manage-certs \- manage certificate infrastructure for a GVM installation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gvm-manage-certs
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gvm-manage-certs
manages the certificate infrastructure for a GVM installation.
The certificate infrastructure enables GVM daemons to communicate in a
secure manner and is used for authentication and authorization before
establishing TLS connections between the daemons.
.PP
The GVM certificate infrastructure consists of a certificate authority (CA)
which is trusted by all GVM daemons.
This CA is then used to sign certificates used by the various daemons.
The certificates can be divided into two use cases:
.IP \(bu 3
Server certificates, primarily used for authentication
.IP \(bu 3
Client certificates, primarily used for authorization
.PP
.B gvm-manage-certs
can perform an automatic creation of a default certificate infrastructure for a
standard GVM installation.
It can also verify an existing infrastructure and perform various certificate
related tasks to support the setup of a more complex infrastructure.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Certificate infrastructure management"
.TP
\fB\-a\fR
Automatically set up default infrastructure for GVM
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
Verify existing GVM certificate infrastructure
.TP
\fB\-C\fR
Create a certificate authority (CA)
.TP
\fB\-I\fR
Install a CA certificate and key, use with the GVM_CERT_DIR and
GVM_CERT_PREFIX environment variables
.TP
\fB\-i\fR
Install a certificate and key, use with the GVM_CERT_DIR and
GVM_CERT_PREFIX environment variables
.TP
\fB\-n\fR
Install a ca certificate, use with the GVM_CERT_DIR and GVM_CERT_PREFIX
environment variables
.TP
\fB\-R\fR
Create a request for a certificate with the CA basic constraint true
.TP
\fB\-r\fR
Create a certificate request, use with -E or -L and the
GVM_CERT_PREFIX environment variable
.TP
\fB\-c\fR
Create a certificate request and sign it, use with -E or -L and the
GVM_CERT_PREFIX environment variable
.TP
\fB\-S\fR
Sign a certificate request, use with the GVM_CERT_DIR and GVM_CERT_PREFIX
environment variables
.TP
\fB\-s\fR
Create a self signed certificate, use with -E or -L and the
GVM_CERT_PREFIX environment variable
.TP
\fB\-f\fR
Force overwriting of existing files
.SS "Certificate options"
.TP
\fB\-E\fR
Create a server certificate.
This sets the appropriate key usage constraints for a server certificate.
.TP
\fB\-L\fR
Create a client certificate.
This sets the appropriate key usage constraints for a client certificate.
.TP
\fB\-A\fR
Skip CA generation in automatic mode.
This automatically (re-)generates server and client certificates, but keeps the CA certificate.
.SS "Output control"
.TP
\fB\-d\fR
Print debug output
.TP
\fB\-v\fR
Print verbose messages
.TP
\fB\-q\fR
Be quiet, only print error messages
.SS "Configuration"
\fB\-e\fR \fIfile\fR
Read configuration from
.IR file
(see below for configuration details)
.SS "Other options"
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
Print help
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The requested operation was successfully performed.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred, the requested operation could not be performed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
All the below certificate generation options can be set either through
the configuration file or through environment variables.
.SS Static variables
These are variables you are likely to want to set in your profile or
the configuration file.
.TP
GVM_CERTIFICATE_LIFETIME
Days until the certificate will expire
.TP
GVM_CERTIFICATE_HOSTNAME
Name to use for the certificate, if not set,
.B gvm-manage-certs
will try to guess the hostname and default to "localhost" if it cannot
be guessed
.TP
GVM_CERTIFICATE_SIGNALG
Hash algorithm to use for signing
.TP
GVM_CERTIFICATE_KEYSIZE
Size in bits of the generated key, if not set,
.B gvm-manage-certs
will take the default from GVM_CERTIFICATE_SECPARAM (see below)
.TP
GVM_CERTIFICATE_SECPARAM
GnuTLS security level [low|medium|high|ultra]
.SS Dynamic variables
These are variables you are likely to want to set for individual
invocations of
.BR gvm-manage-certs .
.TP
GVM_CERT_DIR
Directory where keys and certificates are stored before installation,
this directory has to exist and be writable; if not set,
.B gvm-manage-certs
will use a temporary directory
.TP
GVM_CERT_PREFIX
Prefix for certificate filename (e.g. "server", "client")
.PP
For a complete list of environment variables, please refer to the
example configuration file included in the documentation.
.SH "DECOUPLED OPERATIONS"
If you need a certificate from a certificate authority run by
.B gvm-manage-certs
that you don't control create a request with for instance

.EX
runuser -u gvm -- env GVM_CERT_PREFIX=server gvm-manage-certs -r -E
.EE

adjusting the prefix and choice of
.BR -E " or " -L
to your current requirements.
.B gvm-manage-certs
tells you where it wrote the key, request and certtool template.
Transfer (with integrity guarantees) the request and certtool template
to the certificate authority. If your request is approved you will get
back a certificate and ca certificate. Place the certificate alongside
the key that was generated by the last command, make it readable to
the gvm user and import it with something like

.EX
runuser -u gvm -- env GVM_CERT_PREFIX=server GVM_CERT_DIR=/tmp/gvmca-charsalad gvm-manage-certs -i
.EE

passing the same prefix you used with the
.B -r
command and the dir where it wrote the key. If you had not already
imported the ca certificate place that as well alongside the other
files, make it readable to the gvm user and import it with

.EX
runuser -u gvm -- env GVM_CERT_PREFIX=ca GVM_CERT_DIR=/tmp/gvmca-charsalad gvm-manage-certs -n
.EE

passing the dir where you have placed the ca certificate. (If the user
running
.B ospd-openvas
on your system is not
.I gvm
you will have to adjust the
.BR -u " argument to " runuser
above.)

If you run a certificate authority with
.B gvm-manage-certs
and want to sign a certificate for someone else ask them for a
certificate request and certtool template, verify their integrity,
review them and if they look like you expect (or close enough to what
your own
.B gvm-manage-certs
would produce) then sign the request with

.EX
GVM_CERT_PREFIX=server GVM_CERT_DIR=. gvm-manage-certs -S
.EE

passing what preceeds request.pem in the request as prefix and where
you placed the request and template as dir.
.B gvm-manage-certs
tells you where it wrote the certificate, send it back to the
requester along with the cacert
.RI "(typically " "/var/lib/gvm/CA/cacert.pem" ")."
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR openvas (8),
.BR gvmd (8),
.BR gsad (8)
